        ":question/problem": "Given the function  $y=2\\log_{3}x-4\\operatorname{ln}x+5{e}^{x},$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Give an exact answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\log_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\log_{3}x-4\\operatorname{ln}x+5{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}-\\frac{4}{x}+5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}-\\frac{4}{x}+5{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}3}-\\frac{4}{1}+5{e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}3}-4+5\\cdot e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
